WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.760
Welcome back to unintended consequences, episode 69 for July 25th, 2026.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:11.900
We're looking at a case today where the people involved were doing their best with the information they had.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:17.600
Uber set out to free drivers from taxi medallions with flexible, well-paid work

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:22.180
and left millions earning below minimum wage in algorithm-driven jobs.

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:28.860
In the summer of 2010, a small team in San Francisco began dispatching black-town cars

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:32.240
through an app that let riders request a ride with a few taps.

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:37.900
The founders believed they were replacing a broken system of medallions, radio dispatch

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:41.560
and unpredictable fares with something cleaner and more efficient.

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:47.480
Within 15 years, the same platform would manage millions of drivers who's take home pay,

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:52.680
after fuel, maintenance, and unpaid time often fell below local minimum wages

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:54.780
in the cities where Uber operated.

00:00:55.580 --> 00:01:00.120
Travis Kalanick and Garrett Camp started the company after struggling to hail a cab

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:02.760
on a snowy night in Paris in 2008.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:06.860
They saw the familiar frustrations of American taxi markets,

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:12.700
medallion systems that limited supply, drivers who leased vehicles at high daily rates,

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:16.420
and riders who faced long waits or arbitrary refusals.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:21.880
At the time, smartphone GPS and mobile payments offered a technical fix

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:26.460
that could match supply and demand in real time without the old gatekeepers.

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:32.400
Early investors and city officials in San Francisco viewed the idea as a way to increase

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:36.800
vehicle utilization and reduce the need for large fleets, sitting idle.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:43.560
The model treated drivers as independent contractors who could log on or off whenever they chose,

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:48.560
an arrangement that matched the flexibility many part-time workers said they wanted.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:53.700
Regulators initially treated the service as a premium black car option

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:59.720
rather than a direct competitor to yellow cabs, which lowered the political temperature at launch.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:07.480
Uber cab began operations in San Francisco in May 2010 with a handful of licensed limousines.

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:12.760
By 2012, the company had expanded to New York and several other cities,

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:18.720
raised hundreds of millions in venture capital, and introduced lower priced Uber X services

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:21.040
that recruited ordinary car owners.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:25.660
Proponents pointed to shorter wait times and lower fares for riders.

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:31.880
A 2014 study commissioned by Uber itself reported that drivers earned a median

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:37.460
of roughly $17 to $19 per hour before expenses in several major markets.

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:41.940
Skeptics, including some labor economists and taxi regulators,

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:46.880
warned that treating drivers as contractors would shift vehicle costs and downtime

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:50.380
onto workers while the platform captured the data advantage.

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:54.840
City Councils in Boston and Washington, DC held hearings

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:59.320
but largely allowed the service to grow under existing four higher rules.

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:05.060
The company's valuation climbed past $50 billion by 2015

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:09.080
on the strength of rapid rider adoption and driver recruitment.

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:13.760
As driver numbers grew faster than rider demand in many markets,

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:18.100
the algorithm adjusted payouts downward to keep utilization high.

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:24.740
A 2018 study by the MIT Center for Energy and Environmental Policy Research

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:29.860
estimated that after subtracting vehicle expenses, net hourly earnings

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:35.540
for Uber and Lyft drivers in several large US cities fell to between $8 and $11.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:42.160
That figure sat below prevailing minimum wages once unpaid time between rides was included.

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:46.440
Because drivers bore depreciation, insurance, and maintenance,

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:50.640
the platform could lower fares without directly cutting its own margins.

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:55.840
The same real-time data that matched trips also allowed the company to predict

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:00.900
and manage driver availability, reducing the value of individual flexibility.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:06.960
Drivers who logged off during low-demand periods found it harder to reach weekly income targets.

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:13.020
In response to reclassification efforts, Uber and its allies spent more than $200 million

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:20.400
on lobbying and ballot campaigns between 2015 and 2022, most notably defeating

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:25.040
California's AB5 through Proposition 22 in 2020.

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:30.500
The outcome locked in contractor status in that state while similar fights continued

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:33.020
in the UK, Spain, and Australia.

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:38.000
Second order effects included increased traffic congestion in dense cores

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:42.880
as more vehicles chased fares and a growing population of full-time drivers

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:47.480
who lacked unemployment insurance or workers' compensation when injuries occurred.

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:51.940
Platforms responded to earnings pressure by tightening acceptance rates

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:56.280
and introducing quests that required drivers to complete a set number of trips

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:57.920
within narrow-time windows.

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:01.840
Further constraining the flexibility originally advertised.

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:07.860
After Proposition 22 passed, Uber and similar companies continued to face lawsuits

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:11.000
and regulatory pressure in Europe and parts of Asia.

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:16.320
Some cities introduced minimum-per-trip pay rules or caps on vehicle numbers.

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:23.220
New York City's 2018 minimum pay standard for app drivers produced modest earnings gains

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:28.220
but also prompted companies to reduce driver supply through stricter vehicle requirements.

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:35.880
Uber went public in 2019 and later reported its first profitable year in 2023,

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:42.700
yet driver turnover remained high and recruitment increasingly relied on new immigrant communities.

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:48.520
No large-scale reclassification to employee status has occurred in the United States.

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:54.300
Instead, the contractor model has spread to delivery and other platform services.

00:05:54.940 --> 00:06:00.280
Estimates from the Bureau of Labor Statistics suggest that roughly 1.5 million workers

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:04.800
performed driving or delivery tasks through apps as of 2023,

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:10.380
with earnings distributions that remain heavily skewed toward the top decile of drivers.

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:15.020
Incentive systems that treat labor supply as infinitely elastic

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:19.980
tend to drive compensation toward the marginal cost of the next available worker

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:23.000
rather than the cost of sustaining the workforce.

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:30.400
When platforms control pricing, matching, and performance data while shifting capital costs outward,

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:34.560
flexibility for the firm becomes precarity for the individual.

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:40.520
Regulators and founders alike underestimated how quickly the original balance between

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:46.080
rider convenience and driver earnings would shift once growth targets dominated decision-making.

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:51.020
The pattern invites a practical question for any new platform or policy.

00:06:51.620 --> 00:06:56.060
What happens to compensation and risk when the number of participants doubles

00:06:56.060 --> 00:06:59.320
and the data advantage remains with the intermediary?

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:01.260
We'll leave it there for today.

00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:03.600
Thanks for thinking through this one with me.

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:08.020
If you know someone who loves a good cautionary tale, send it their way,

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:09.920
and I'll see you tomorrow with another.

